1
00:00:00,370 --> 00:00:02,325
A classic
exam trap.

2
00:00:02,365 --> 00:00:04,224
Which sentence
is correct?

3
00:00:07,970 --> 00:00:09,286
Most students
pick ‘two

4
00:00:09,326 --> 00:00:11,758
breads’, because
two means plural.

5
00:00:11,798 --> 00:00:13,072
That’s the trap.

6
00:00:13,703 --> 00:00:14,696
Here’s the test:

7
00:00:14,736 --> 00:00:16,219
how do we
buy bread?

8
00:00:16,259 --> 00:00:17,801
Not in pieces
we count,

9
00:00:17,841 --> 00:00:20,121
but by the loaf
or the slice.

10
00:00:20,161 --> 00:00:21,266
Bread is measured,

11
00:00:21,306 --> 00:00:23,797
so bread itself
is uncountable.

12
00:00:24,437 --> 00:00:26,252
So bread
never takes s.

13
00:00:26,292 --> 00:00:27,827
That kills
option A.

14
00:00:27,867 --> 00:00:29,501
And option D
says ‘two bread’,

15
00:00:29,541 --> 00:00:31,038
with nothing
to count.

16
00:00:31,078 --> 00:00:32,251
Wrong too.

17
00:00:32,903 --> 00:00:34,905
So we count
the loaf instead.

18
00:00:34,945 --> 00:00:36,615
One loaf,
two loaves.

19
00:00:36,655 --> 00:00:39,152
Option B forgot
the plural of loaf.

20
00:00:39,192 --> 00:00:40,499
The answer is C:

21
00:00:40,539 --> 00:00:42,541
I need two
loaves of bread.

22
00:00:43,470 --> 00:00:45,131
Logic, not rules.

23
00:00:45,171 --> 00:00:47,324
Comment ‘logic’
for the next one.

